---
import SummerLayout from '../../../layouts/SummerLayout.astro';
import SpokePagination from '../../../components/summer-reading/SpokePagination.astro';
import '../../../styles/pachinko-zainichi.css';

const imgBase = '/images/summer-reading/pachinko/zainichi';
---

<SummerLayout title="Japan and the Zainichi — Pachinko · The Living Atlas" section="pachinko" spoke="history">

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 1 — Annexation and Dispossession · photo · steady · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--photo" data-field="photo" data-tempo="steady" data-ground="cream" data-text="pachinko" data-screen-label="Zone 1 · Annexation and Dispossession">
    <div class="sr-zone-field sr-zone-photo-stack" data-stages="3" aria-hidden="true">
      <figure class="sr-photo-stage" data-stage="stage-1876">
        <img class="sr-zone-photo sr-zone-photo--wide" src={`${imgBase}/history/ganghwa-gatling-1876.jpg`} alt="Four Gatling guns and a line of Japanese troops drawn up before a tiled-roof building at Ganghwa, 1876 Kuroda mission." />
        <figcaption class="sr-zone-caption"><span class="sr-stage-year">1876</span>The Treaty of Ganghwa forces open three Korean ports — the first concession in a methodical, three-decade dismantling. <em>Gatling guns staged by Japanese troops at Ganghwa during the Kuroda mission, 1876. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="sr-photo-stage" data-stage="stage-1910">
        <img class="sr-zone-photo sr-zone-photo--wide" src={`${imgBase}/history/annexation-power-of-attorney-1910.jpg`} alt="The 1910 general power of attorney, brush-written in Korean with a large red imperial seal at left, delegating authority to Yi Wan-yong and sealed by Emperor Sunjong." />
        <figcaption class="sr-zone-caption"><span class="sr-stage-year">1910</span>Formal annexation. The power of attorney above — sealed by Emperor Sunjong — handed full authority to Prime Minister Yi Wan-yong to conclude the treaty; Korea is renamed <em>Chōsen</em> and placed under a military Governor-General. <em>General power of attorney to Yi Wan-yong, signed and sealed by Sunjong, 1910. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="sr-photo-stage" data-stage="stage-1965">
        <img class="sr-zone-photo" src={`${imgBase}/history/normalization-treaty-1965.jpg`} alt="Black-and-white photograph of President Park Chung-hee seated at a desk signing the 1965 normalization treaty at the Blue House, flanked by officials in dark suits." />
        <figcaption class="sr-zone-caption"><span class="sr-stage-year">1965</span>Normalization. President Park Chung-hee signs the Treaty on Basic Relations — settling claims but leaving the annexation's validity deliberately ambiguous, a dispute that remains live today. <em>President Park Chung-hee signing the normalization treaty at the Blue House, December 17, 1965.</em></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="sr-stage-dots" aria-hidden="true">
        <span data-dot="stage-1876"></span><span data-dot="stage-1910"></span><span data-dot="stage-1965"></span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sr-zone-beats">
      <div class="sr-section-opener sr-section-opener--pachinko sr-section-opener--bg-cream">
        <div class="sr-section-opener-motif" data-motif="medallion" data-size="40"></div>
        <span class="sr-section-opener-eyebrow">HOW JAPAN TOOK KOREA</span>
        <h2 class="sr-section-opener-heading">Annexation and Dispossession</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--left sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="stage-1876">
        <div class="sr-beat-card"><p>Korea did not fall quickly. Japan dismantled it methodically over three decades: forced treaties in 1876, protectorate status in 1905, formal annexation in 1910. At each stage, Korean sovereignty was stripped while the appearance of an independent state was maintained long enough for the next concession to be extracted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--right sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="stage-1910">
        <div class="sr-beat-card"><p>Formal annexation came on August 29, 1910. The treaty was signed not by Emperor Sunjong but by Prime Minister Yi Wan-yong, who served as Japan's collaborator and became the most reviled figure in modern Korean history. Japan's Resident-General had suppressed all Korean newspapers in the days before signing to prevent public knowledge of what was being negotiated. Korea was renamed <em>Chōsen</em> and governed by a Governor-General — always a Japanese military officer appointed directly by the Japanese Emperor.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--center sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="stage-1965">
        <div class="sr-beat-card"><p>The annexation's legal validity has been disputed ever since. When Japan and South Korea normalized relations in 1965, the compromise language declared all pre-1910 treaties "already null and void" — deliberately ambiguous about whether they were invalid from the start or merely rescinded. That ambiguity remains a live political dispute: a disagreement, fundamentally, over whether Japan owes a full accounting of what it did.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sr-zone-seam" data-from="cream" data-to="tinted" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-half sr-zone-seam-half--from"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-motif" data-motif="rosette" data-size="44"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-half sr-zone-seam-half--to"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 2 — The Colonial Timeline 1876–1945 · cascade · driving · tinted
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--cascade" data-field="cascade" data-tempo="driving" data-ground="tinted" data-text="pachinko" data-cascade-variant="land" data-screen-label="Zone 2 · The Colonial Timeline">
    <div class="sr-zone-cascade-layout">
      <div class="sr-zone-cascade-rail" aria-hidden="true"><div class="sr-zone-cascade-stem"></div></div>
      <div class="sr-zone-cascade-content">
        <div class="sr-section-opener sr-section-opener--pachinko sr-section-opener--bg-tinted">
          <div class="sr-section-opener-motif" data-motif="medallion" data-size="40"></div>
          <span class="sr-section-opener-eyebrow">THE MECHANISM</span>
          <h2 class="sr-section-opener-heading">Japanese Policy in Korea, 1876–1945</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-cascade-entries">
        <div class="sr-zone-cascade-progress"></div>
        <div class="sr-cascade-entry" data-beat>
          <div class="sr-cascade-pin" aria-hidden="true"><div class="sr-cascade-dot"></div></div>
          <div class="sr-cascade-text"><span class="sr-cascade-year">1876</span><p class="sr-cascade-event"><strong>Treaty of Ganghwa.</strong> Japan forces Korea to open three ports to Japanese trade under an unequal treaty — Korea's first formal step toward colonial subjugation.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cascade-entry" data-beat>
          <div class="sr-cascade-pin" aria-hidden="true"><div class="sr-cascade-dot"></div></div>
          <div class="sr-cascade-text"><span class="sr-cascade-year">1905</span><p class="sr-cascade-event"><strong>Korea becomes a Japanese protectorate.</strong> Following the Russo-Japanese War, Japan controls Korea's foreign policy, military, and civil administration. Formal sovereignty survives in name only.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cascade-entry sr-cascade-entry--highlight" data-beat>
          <div class="sr-cascade-pin" aria-hidden="true"><div class="sr-cascade-dot"></div></div>
          <div class="sr-cascade-text"><span class="sr-cascade-year">1910</span><p class="sr-cascade-event"><strong>Annexation.</strong> Korea is formally absorbed into the Japanese Empire and renamed <em>Chōsen</em>. Korean newspapers are suppressed; the country is placed under a military Governor-General.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cascade-entry" data-beat>
          <div class="sr-cascade-pin" aria-hidden="true"><div class="sr-cascade-dot"></div></div>
          <div class="sr-cascade-text"><span class="sr-cascade-year">1910–1918</span><p class="sr-cascade-event"><strong>The Land Survey.</strong> Japanese property registration displaces Korean farmers under customary tenure, transferring land to Japanese buyers. By the survey's end, approximately 70% of Korean agricultural workers are tenant farmers.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cascade-entry sr-cascade-entry--highlight" data-beat>
          <div class="sr-cascade-pin" aria-hidden="true"><div class="sr-cascade-dot"></div></div>
          <div class="sr-cascade-text"><span class="sr-cascade-year">1919</span><p class="sr-cascade-event"><strong>The March First Movement.</strong> Two million Koreans participate in demonstrations for independence. Japan responds with force — approximately 7,500 killed, 46,000 arrested. Japan shifts from military rule to "cultural rule."</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cascade-entry" data-beat>
          <div class="sr-cascade-pin" aria-hidden="true"><div class="sr-cascade-dot"></div></div>
          <div class="sr-cascade-text"><span class="sr-cascade-year">1937–1942</span><p class="sr-cascade-event"><strong>Wartime assimilation policies.</strong> <em>Kōminka</em> — "imperialization" — intensifies: Korean language banned in schools (1937), the <em>sōshi-kaimei</em> policy requires Korean families to register Japanese-style names (1940), military conscription extended to Korea (1942).</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cascade-entry" data-beat>
          <div class="sr-cascade-pin" aria-hidden="true"><div class="sr-cascade-dot"></div></div>
          <div class="sr-cascade-text"><span class="sr-cascade-year">1939–1945</span><p class="sr-cascade-event"><strong>Labor mobilization.</strong> Hundreds of thousands of Koreans are mobilized for wartime labor in Japan and Japanese-controlled territories — through recruitment, government coordination, and formal conscription. The Zainichi community's origins are built from this migration.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cascade-entry sr-cascade-entry--highlight" data-beat>
          <div class="sr-cascade-pin" aria-hidden="true"><div class="sr-cascade-dot"></div></div>
          <div class="sr-cascade-text"><span class="sr-cascade-year">1945</span><p class="sr-cascade-event"><strong>Surrender and liberation.</strong> Japan surrenders in August 1945. Colonial rule ends. Approximately two million Koreans are living in Japan. Most eventually repatriate; roughly 600,000 remain — and are immediately confronted with a new legal question: who are they now?</p></div>
        </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sr-zone-seam" data-from="tinted" data-to="cream" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-half sr-zone-seam-half--from"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-motif" data-motif="rosette" data-size="44"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-half sr-zone-seam-half--to"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 3 — The San Francisco Moment · ground · still · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="cream" data-text="pachinko" data-screen-label="Zone 3 · From Subjects to Stateless">
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">
      <div class="sr-section-opener sr-section-opener--pachinko sr-section-opener--bg-cream">
        <div class="sr-section-opener-motif" data-motif="medallion" data-size="40"></div>
        <span class="sr-section-opener-eyebrow">1952</span>
        <h2 class="sr-section-opener-heading">From Subjects to Stateless</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="pz-still">
        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>Japan's treatment of the Zainichi community after 1945 is one of the most precisely documented cases of systematic legal dispossession in modern history. The mechanism was not a single dramatic act of exclusion but a series of administrative decisions — each one narrowing the community's legal standing until, in 1952, the process reached its logical conclusion.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="pz-contradiction">
          <div class="pz-ct-grid">
            <div class="pz-ct-panel pz-ct-panel--l">
              <span class="pz-ct-law">Election Law revision · Dec 1945</span>
              <div class="pz-ct-claim">Too <em>Korean</em> to vote</div>
              <div class="pz-ct-sub">Voting rights revoked — Koreans ruled <em>not Japanese.</em></div>
            </div>
            <div class="pz-ct-fulcrum">
              <span class="pz-ct-law pz-ct-law-ghost">·</span>
              <span class="pz-ct-eq">＝</span>
              <span class="pz-ct-same">same<br />people</span>
            </div>
            <div class="pz-ct-panel pz-ct-panel--r">
              <span class="pz-ct-law">School Closure Orders · 1948</span>
              <div class="pz-ct-claim">Too <em>Japanese</em> to self-govern</div>
              <div class="pz-ct-sub">Ethnic schools shut — Koreans ruled <em>Japanese nationals.</em></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="pz-ct-coda">The 1947 Alien Registration Ordinance made the doublethink official: it registered Koreans as foreigners while, on paper, they were still Japanese nationals. The state simply held whichever classification excluded them more.</p>
        </div>

        <figure class="pz-figure pz-figure--inset">
          <img class="pz-figure-img" src={`${imgBase}/history/korean-registration-certificate-1946.jpg`} alt="A 1946 Korean Registration Certificate (朝鮮人登録證) issued by Osaka Prefecture: a worn card printed in Japanese and English, hand-filled with the holder's name, occupation and address, bearing a sepia ID photograph of a man and a red official seal." />
          <figcaption class="pz-figcaption">Korean Registration Certificate issued by Osaka Prefecture, 1946. The certificate identifies its holder as Korean — a legal classification that, within six years, would become the basis for stripping that holder of Japanese nationality entirely. <em>History Museum of J-Koreans (在日韓人歴史資料館), Tokyo.</em></figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="pz-stroke">
          <div class="pz-stroke-meta">MINISTRY OF JUSTICE CIRCULAR NOTICE · <b>19 APRIL 1952</b></div>
          <div class="pz-stroke-big">600,000</div>
          <div class="pz-stroke-line">stripped of Japanese nationality overnight — by administrative circular, not by law.</div>
          <div class="pz-stroke-neg">
            <span>No statute passed</span>
            <span>No individual choice offered</span>
          </div>
          <div class="pz-stroke-cite">A notice from the Director-General of the Civil Affairs Bureau instructed officials that, when the San Francisco Peace Treaty took force nine days later, all Koreans would cease to be Japanese nationals. <em>(Iwasawa, International Law, Human Rights, and Japanese Law, Oxford University Press, 1998, pp. 130–31.)</em></div>
        </div>

        <div class="pz-statband" role="img" aria-label="In 1952, 61 percent of the Zainichi community was unemployed; day laboring at 6.6 percent was the single largest recorded occupation; and public-sector jobs, insurance, pension and housing were all closed to non-citizens.">
          <div class="pz-stat-cell"><div class="pz-stat-num">61<small>%</small></div><div class="pz-stat-cap"><strong>Unemployed</strong>in the Zainichi community</div></div>
          <div class="pz-stat-cell"><div class="pz-stat-num">6.6<small>%</small></div><div class="pz-stat-cap"><strong>Day laboring</strong>the single largest recorded occupation</div></div>
          <div class="pz-stat-cell"><div class="pz-stat-num">0</div><div class="pz-stat-cap"><strong>Public-sector jobs</strong>insurance, pension &amp; housing — all closed to non-citizens</div></div>
        </div>
        <p class="pz-statband-foot">Exclusion was comprehensive and immediate. <strong>Pachinko was one of the few economic margins left open.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sr-zone-seam" data-from="cream" data-to="tinted" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-half sr-zone-seam-half--from"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-motif" data-motif="rosette" data-size="44"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-half sr-zone-seam-half--to"></div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 4 — The Legal Arc 1945–1992 · STATUS (map variant) · driving · tinted
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--map" data-field="map" data-tempo="driving" data-ground="tinted" data-text="pachinko" data-screen-label="Zone 4 · The Legal Arc, 1945–1992">
    <div class="pz-pinrail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-field sr-zone-map-field sr-zone-status-field" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg class="sr-status-diagram" viewBox="0 0 700 470" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" role="img" aria-label="A stepped chart of the Zainichi legal condition, 1952–2000, climbing toward but never reaching a full-citizenship ceiling">
        <text class="sd-eyebrow" x="92" y="50">THE LEGAL CONDITION, 1945–2000</text>

        <line class="sd-ceil" x1="92" y1="104" x2="690" y2="104" />
        <text class="sd-ceil-label" x="648" y="95" text-anchor="end">FULL CITIZENSHIP · SECURE BELONGING</text>

        <line class="sd-tick" x1="92" y1="104" x2="92" y2="392" opacity="0.6" />
        <line class="sd-axis" x1="92" y1="392" x2="664" y2="392" />
        <path d="M664 392 l -7 -3.5 l 0 7 z" fill="var(--color-ink)" />

        <g>
          <line class="sd-tick" x1="92"  y1="388" x2="92"  y2="396" /><text class="sd-year" x="92"  y="410" text-anchor="middle">1945</text>
          <line class="sd-tick" x1="159" y1="388" x2="159" y2="396" /><text class="sd-year sd-year-active" x="159" y="410" text-anchor="middle">1952</text>
          <line class="sd-tick" x1="284" y1="388" x2="284" y2="396" /><text class="sd-year sd-year-active" x="284" y="410" text-anchor="middle">1965</text>
          <line class="sd-tick" x1="448" y1="388" x2="448" y2="396" /><text class="sd-year sd-year-active" x="448" y="410" text-anchor="middle">1982</text>
          <line class="sd-tick" x1="534" y1="388" x2="534" y2="396" /><text class="sd-year sd-year-active" x="534" y="410" text-anchor="middle">1991</text>
          <line class="sd-tick" x1="621" y1="388" x2="621" y2="396" /><text class="sd-year sd-year-active" x="621" y="410" text-anchor="middle">2000</text>
        </g>

        <text class="sd-foot" x="378" y="442" text-anchor="middle">Each step is a reform the community organized and won. The dashed line — full citizenship — is the ceiling.</text>

        <g data-show-from="status-1952" opacity="0">
          <polygon points="159,392 159,372 284,372 284,392" fill="#C4537A" opacity="0.5" />
          <path class="sd-tread" d="M159 392 L159 372 L284 372" />
          <circle cx="159" cy="372" r="5" fill="#C4537A" stroke="#F6F1E8" stroke-width="1.4" />
          <text class="sd-cond" x="159" y="360" text-anchor="start">Stateless</text>
        </g>

        <g data-show-from="status-1965" opacity="0">
          <polygon points="284,392 284,300 448,300 448,392" fill="#C4722A" opacity="0.5" />
          <path class="sd-tread" d="M284 372 L284 300 L448 300" />
          <circle cx="284" cy="300" r="5" fill="#C4722A" stroke="#F6F1E8" stroke-width="1.4" />
          <text class="sd-cond" x="284" y="288" text-anchor="middle">Special residency</text>
        </g>

        <g data-show-from="status-1982" opacity="0">
          <polygon points="448,392 448,248 534,248 534,392" fill="#D4A840" opacity="0.5" />
          <path class="sd-tread" d="M448 300 L448 248 L534 248" />
          <circle cx="448" cy="248" r="5" fill="#D4A840" stroke="#F6F1E8" stroke-width="1.4" />
          <text class="sd-cond" x="448" y="236" text-anchor="middle">Broader pathway</text>
        </g>

        <g data-show-from="status-1991" opacity="0">
          <polygon points="534,392 534,192 621,192 621,392" fill="#3FA9A0" opacity="0.5" />
          <path class="sd-tread" d="M534 248 L534 192 L621 192" />
          <circle cx="534" cy="192" r="5" fill="#3FA9A0" stroke="#F6F1E8" stroke-width="1.4" />
          <text class="sd-cond" x="534" y="180" text-anchor="middle">Unified status</text>
        </g>

        <g data-show-from="status-2000" opacity="0">
          <polygon points="621,392 621,174 640,174 640,392" fill="#2AA8A0" opacity="0.5" />
          <path class="sd-tread" d="M621 192 L621 174 L640 174" />
          <circle cx="621" cy="174" r="5" fill="#2AA8A0" stroke="#F6F1E8" stroke-width="1.4" />
          <line x1="640" y1="174" x2="688" y2="174" stroke="#2AA8A0" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3 4" opacity="0.55" />
          <text class="sd-persist" x="694" y="190" text-anchor="end">↳ to the present</text>
          <line x1="636" y1="104" x2="636" y2="174" stroke="#C4537A" stroke-width="1.3" />
          <line x1="631" y1="104" x2="641" y2="104" stroke="#C4537A" stroke-width="1.3" />
          <line x1="631" y1="174" x2="641" y2="174" stroke="#C4537A" stroke-width="1.3" />
          <text class="sd-annot" x="628" y="131" text-anchor="end">citizenship —</text>
          <text class="sd-annot" x="628" y="147" text-anchor="end">never granted</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <div class="sr-zone-beats">
      <div class="sr-section-opener sr-section-opener--pachinko sr-section-opener--bg-tinted">
        <div class="sr-section-opener-motif" data-motif="medallion" data-size="40"></div>
        <span class="sr-section-opener-eyebrow">FORTY-SEVEN YEARS</span>
        <h2 class="sr-section-opener-heading">The Legal Arc, 1945–1992</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--left sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="status-1952">
        <div class="sr-beat-card"><p>The legal status of the Zainichi community did not stabilize after 1952 — it remained in flux for four more decades, improving incrementally through political campaigns the community organized and sustained over generations. Each milestone below is a reform that was fought for; none of them resolved the fundamental condition.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--right sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="status-1965">
        <div class="sr-beat-card"><p>In 1955, fingerprinting was required of all registered foreigners. In 1965, the Japan–South Korea normalization treaty gave South Korea-affiliated Zainichi a "special permanent residency" — legal security, but not citizenship. Those affiliated with North Korea, or with neither state, remained in legal limbo.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--left sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="status-1982">
        <div class="sr-beat-card"><p>In 1982, Japan ratified international human rights covenants that required extending permanent residency more broadly. For the first time, Zainichi Koreans with North Korean affiliation — or no affiliation — had a pathway to legal stability.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--right sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="status-1991">
        <div class="sr-beat-card"><p>The 1985 citizenship law amendment allowed matrilineal transfer for the first time. In 1991, all Korean permanent residents were unified under a single legal category — eliminating a two-tier system that had divided the community since 1965.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--center sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="status-2000">
        <div class="sr-beat-card"><p>The fingerprinting requirement — which had been used as an enforcement tool against the community since 1955 — was partially abolished in 1991–92 for South Korea-affiliated residents. Full abolition for all long-term foreign residents did not come until April 2000. And still, the ceiling above was never reached: Japan extends no citizenship to the Zainichi as a community. A path to individual naturalization exists and many have taken it — but as a birthright, belonging remains, to this day, a gap.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="pz-zone-tail">
    <figure class="pz-figure pz-figure--portrait">
      <img class="pz-figure-img" src={`${imgBase}/history/fingerprinting-protest-1984.jpg`} alt="A 1984 street protest photographed from above: a long column of Zainichi Korean marchers in white caps carry placards beside a sound truck, led by a giant inflatable hand whose raised thumb bears a fingerprint whorl — a protest against the Alien Registration Act's fingerprinting requirement." />
      <figcaption class="pz-figcaption">A protest march against the Alien Registration Act, organized by Zainichi Korean youth and women's groups, October 1984. The giant thumb — marked with a fingerprint whorl — protests the law's fingerprinting requirement; the campaign spanned nearly three decades before partial abolition in 1991–1992. <em>History Museum of J-Koreans (在日韓人歴史資料館), Tokyo.</em></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="sr-zone-seam" data-from="tinted" data-to="cream" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-half sr-zone-seam-half--from"></div>
    <div class="sr-zone-seam-motif" data-motif="rosette" data-size="44"></div>
