  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 5 — The Pachinko Industry · ground · still · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground sr-scrolly-zone--pachinko-feature" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="cream" data-text="pachinko" data-screen-label="Zone 5 · The Pachinko Industry">

    <div class="pz-pi-hero">
      <img class="pz-pi-hero-img" src={`${imgBase}/pachinko/parlor-boom-era.png`} alt="A crowded boom-era pachinko parlor: long rows of machines, players seated under hanging lights and vintage signage, staff carrying red trays of steel balls through a smoky hall." />
      <div class="pz-pi-hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="pz-pi-hero-scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="pz-pi-hero-cap">
        <span class="pz-pi-hero-eyebrow">EXCLUSION AS FOUNDATION</span>
        <h2 class="pz-pi-hero-title">The Pachinko Industry</h2>
        <p class="pz-pi-credit">A boom-era parlor floor — the industry the community did not choose.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pz-still">
      <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
        <p>The pachinko industry's relationship to the Zainichi community is a direct consequence of the discrimination described above — and a precise illustration of how systematic exclusion shapes economic life. Zainichi Koreans did not choose pachinko. They entered it because the system left them few other options, and stayed because, when others left, they could not.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pz-pi-strip">
      <figure class="pz-pi-plate">
        <div class="pz-pi-plate-img"><img src={`${imgBase}/pachinko/machine-face-1960s.jpg`} alt="The face of a restored 1960s Shōraku pachinko machine: a pale blue speckled circular playfield studded with brass pins in fan patterns, maroon-and-gold spinner targets, brass tulip catchers, and a wood cabinet with a metal ball tray and brass launch handle." /></div>
        <figcaption class="pz-pi-plate-cap"><span class="num">main_037</span><span>Shōraku machine, 1960s</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="pz-pi-plate">
        <div class="pz-pi-plate-img"><img src={`${imgBase}/pachinko/steel-balls.jpg`} alt="An open palm cupping roughly thirty polished steel pachinko balls, their mirror-bright surfaces catching the light." /></div>
        <figcaption class="pz-pi-plate-cap"><span class="num">bl_004</span><span>Steel balls (玉)</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="pz-pi-plate">
        <div class="pz-pi-plate-img"><img src={`${imgBase}/pachinko/ball-renting-machine.jpg`} alt="A polished steel pachinko ball-renting machine: a glass hopper brimming with steel balls above a hand-crank dispenser and a catch tray, its panels labeled in Japanese (パチンコ液箱)." /></div>
        <figcaption class="pz-pi-plate-cap"><span class="num">main_041</span><span>Ball-renting machine</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="pz-pi-stripcaption">
      <p class="pz-figcaption">Equipment from the era of rapid Zainichi Korean ownership growth following the 1954 contraction. <em>History Museum of J-Koreans (在日韓人歴史資料館), Tokyo.</em></p>
    </div>

    <div class="pz-still">
      <div class="sr-beat-card sr-beat-card--termcard">
        <div class="sr-beat-term-header">
          <span class="sr-beat-term-original">パチンコ</span>
          <div class="sr-beat-term-meta">
            <span class="sr-beat-term-romaji">pachinko</span>
            <span class="sr-beat-term-gloss">vertical pinball machine; the industry built around it</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-beat-term-body"><p>Gambling is formally prohibited in Japan. Pachinko circumvents this through a prize-exchange mechanism: players win tokens or prizes that are then exchanged for cash at separate kiosks nearby. This quasi-legal structure made Japanese investors reluctant to commit to the industry. When a 1954 regulatory change caused a significant contraction — the number of parlors collapsed from 50,000 to 9,000 — Japanese owners could leave for other work. Zainichi Koreans, excluded from most formal employment, had no such exit. They stayed. As the industry recovered and expanded, Korean ownership grew dramatically.</p></div>
      </div>

      <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
        <p>By the 1990s, pachinko had become a 19 trillion yen annual industry — roughly twice the export revenues of Japan's entire car industry. Zainichi Koreans accounted for approximately 90% of sales.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pz-pi-stat" role="img" aria-label="Pachinko's annual revenue in the early 1990s was roughly twice Japan's total automotive export revenue.">
        <span class="pz-info-eyebrow pz-pi-stat-eyebrow">ANNUAL REVENUE · EARLY 1990s</span>
        <div class="pz-bar-row">
          <div class="pz-bar-label"><span class="pz-bar-name">Pachinko industry</span><span class="pz-bar-val">≈ ¥19 trillion / year</span></div>
          <div class="pz-bar-track"><div class="pz-bar-fill pz-bar-fill--pachinko"></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pz-bar-row">
          <div class="pz-bar-label"><span class="pz-bar-name">Japan's total automotive exports</span><span class="pz-bar-val">≈ ¥9.5 trillion / year</span></div>
          <div class="pz-bar-track"><div class="pz-bar-fill pz-bar-fill--auto"></div></div>
        </div>
        <p class="pz-info-mult">≈ 2× the export revenue of Japan's entire car industry — and Zainichi Koreans accounted for roughly 90% of sales.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pz-pi-shadow">
      <img class="pz-pi-shadow-img" src={`${imgBase}/pachinko/parlor-night-exterior.png`} alt="A lone pachinko parlor glowing across a deserted, rain-slicked street at night; its neon パチンコ sign burns against the fog while a single figure walks away into the dark." />
      <div class="pz-pi-shadow-scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="pz-pi-shadow-inner">
        <span class="pz-pi-shadow-eyebrow">WHAT PROSPERITY CANNOT BUY</span>
        <h3 class="pz-pi-shadow-title">The industry Japanese society disdained, built into an economic foundation the community had not chosen.</h3>
        <p>The stigma attached to pachinko — its association with gambling, quasi-legality, and Korean identity — was produced by the same discrimination that made Korean participation necessary. In <em>Pachinko</em>, this dynamic runs through every generation: Mozasu's success in the industry is real and hard-won; what it cannot buy is the respect the dominant culture withholds on principle.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="pz-pi-shadow-credit">Atmospheric rendering · a boom-era parlor after hours</p>
    </div>
  </section>

