        <div class="sr-beat-card sr-beat-card--termcard">
          <div class="sr-beat-term-header">
            <span class="sr-beat-term-original">在日韓国・朝鮮人</span>
            <div class="sr-beat-term-meta">
              <span class="sr-beat-term-romaji">Zainichi Kankoku / Chōsenjin</span>
              <span class="sr-beat-term-gloss">"Korean residents in Japan"</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="sr-beat-term-body"><p>The term <em>Zainichi</em> (在日) — literally "residing in Japan" — became the community's common self-designation across both political affiliations. Its neutrality was the point: it named a condition (living in Japan) rather than a political loyalty (South or North Korea) or a legal status (alien). In the novel, the word carries the weight of everything the community built in the space between those categories.</p></div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>Hansu's dismissal of both organizations in the novel — his observation that neither group's leadership ever actually intends to return to Korea — reflects something Min Jin Lee found repeatedly in her research: the Zainichi community was often clear-eyed about the gap between organizational political rhetoric and the reality of people's lives. The organizations were necessary. They were also, for many in the community, beside the point.</p>
        </div>
